# Approved reduction scope and invariant mapping

## Request and source

The user requested analysis of the attached October 10, 2026 Port Authority package,
then said “write it” after the recommendation to retain one `port-authority` identity
with a small default contract and separately selected advanced controls.

The designated original is
`attached_assets/port-authority_(10.10.2026)_1791743364013.zip`.
The source manifest pins the archive and every original member. Source implementation
occurs at canonical `.agents/skills/port-authority/` only. The input archive is preserved.
This is package authorship, not project implementation/adoption, native task creation,
skill publication, policy activation or live process reclaim.

## Deliberate contract changes

| Original responsibility | Revised boundary |
|---|---|
| Full base/heavy phases in the entry point | Small core workflow; advanced/symptom modules selected only when relevant. |
| Action-capable cleanup interface as ordinary template | Default read-only inventory; action requires leading advanced profile plus genuine checked authority. |
| Named resources, priority and verified reentry exposed together | Single outer lease by default; those features explicitly advanced. |
| Attested exception available through shared admission | All production markers deny core; independent exception is advanced-only. |
| Duplicated tier/ignore/provenance/retry/completion instructions | Failure Gate Unified when adopted, otherwise the verified host contract, owns those rules; no new tiers, ignores or automatic diagnostic retries here. |
| Application-timer/pool/browser/WebSocket guidance in main flow | Symptom-specific reference; no unrelated app audit by default. |

These are approved behavioral boundaries, not claims of semantics-preserving prose
compression. Legacy callers require authorized migration; existing active policy
continues until actual approved cutover.

## Preserved invariants

- Relevant audit before affected changes; genuine scoped disruption authorization.
- Exact socket/process/ancestor/incarnation evidence; protected unrelated targets.
- Invalid/production/disabled/unknown states are not successful cleanup.
- Core has no pre-existing-process signaling or fake approval fallback.
- Advanced reclaim retains host attestation, actual checked grant, exact task/plan/run,
  atomic claim/replay/conflict control, revocation and authoritative outcome storage.
- Core's owned-child teardown and managed host stop interfaces are not reclaim grants.
- Complete actual caller-to-canonical-lock coverage; no invented hierarchy/remote exclusion.
- Explicit safe finite queue/execution/termination limits; parent accounting remains host-owned.
- Registered private startup gate and independent watchdog; no raw-zero timeout masking.
- Owned descendant/incarnation cleanup and verified quiescence before release.
- Retained uncertain/error/legacy/launch-gap state; no heartbeat-age takeover or blind unlink.
- Two consecutive authorized acceptance runs for activated controls; fixtures do not prove deployed wiring.
- Stage-specific expected-versus-actual outcomes; correct negative cases do not excuse missing requirements.
- Existing selected/required independent review remains separate; scoped repair
  renews affected proof only while that review remains selected/required. There is
  no default reviewer prerequisite; runtime watchdog independence is unchanged.
- Canonical sources/deliverables outside `.local`; disposable runtime/fixture state is distinct.

## Regression Guard

The starting archive's runtime scripts match the pre-edit canonical runtime modules.
The original advanced behavior-test tail also matches; the initial local policy-text
tests differ from the uploaded revision and are replaced against the approved reduced
contract, not treated as immutable formatting requirements.

Retain all original advanced runtime behavior cases. Adapt only their private profile
selection and nested fixture routing; do not drop protections to make tests pass.
Add actual default-profile regressions for read-only busy/free discovery, invalid and
production inputs, denied action/manifest/reentry/priority/resource options, unconfigured
adapters, raw exits, same-file exclusion, queue expiry, finite limits and execution expiry.

Replace obsolete phase-count, package-size and retry wording assertions with complete
core requirement IDs C01–C12 and explicit stage/advanced/host-evidence boundaries.
These are static contract checks, not proof of installed application behavior.

## Scope excluded from this authoring

No real project was connected or changed. No deployment, managed service, live reclaim,
host authorization provider, policy adoption, independent reviewer, native task API,
cron/hook or current-snapshot app completion was implemented or asserted.
Later installation must read the entire original/revised scope and preserve required
file-only confirmation, resource verification and actual app acceptance separately.
